General
-------
- Keep everything minimal but sufficient
- Focus on one concept at a time
- Each branch builds on previous concepts
- Provide boilerplate, leave learning gaps
- Make them create files only for core learning (e.g., first .py)
- Use modern Python practices:
  - Type hints
  - f-strings
  - Async where appropriate
  - Context managers
- Follow PEP 8 and clean code practices
- Problem-based learning approach

README.md
---------
- Fresh content each branch
- Only 3 sections: Problem, Tasks, Check
- Real-world context in problem
- Clear, specific tasks
- Verifiable check steps
- No teaching material

Sample:
```
Step 01 – Hello, Health Tracker
===========================

💭 Problem / Pain
-----------------
Real-world context for the task

🛠️ Tasks
--------
- [ ] Create/modify specific files
- [ ] Implement specific features

✅ Check
--------
Clear verification steps
```

Notes.md
--------
- Prepend new sections, never modify previous
- 5-10 focused bullets per section
- Max 50 chars per line
- Active voice, present tense
- No solutions, only guidance
- Visual formatting:
  - Consistent emoji usage
  - Align similar elements
  - Group related points
- Code examples:
  - Show good vs bad practices
  - Use ✅/❌ markers
  - Keep aligned and readable
  - Include brief comments
- Links and references:
  - Group at section end
  - Use short names [Docs]
  - Link to specific sections

Sample:
```
📝 Notes
--------
- 🔑 Key Concept  
    Brief, clear explanation.
    [Docs](mdc:https:/specific-section-link)

- ▶️ Usage  
    ```python
    good_code()      # ✅ Clear, simple
    bad_code();      # ❌ Poor style
    ```

- 💡 Tips  
    Key points to remember.
    [Guide](mdc:https:/specific-guide-section)
```
